        "original_id": "2ab44627-7f31-57b9-bee2-dec3847bab68",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: David Thewlis, Paul Greengrass, Martin Allen, William Shakespeare\n- Characters: King Duncan, Macbeth, Malcolm, Donalbain\n- Works: Naked, Dragonheart, Kingdom of Heaven, The Boy in the Striped Pyjamas, The Theory of Everything, Black Beauty, Macbeth, Wonder Woman, Resurrected\n- Events: Cannes Film Festival\n\nFacts:\n- In 1989, the drama film \"Resurrected\" was directed by Paul Greengrass in his directorial debut, written by Martin Allen, and starred David Thewlis.\n- David Thewlis won the Best Actor award at the Cannes Film Festival for his performance in the film \"Naked.\"\n- David Thewlis had other notable performances in \"Dragonheart,\" \"Kingdom of Heaven,\" \"The Boy in the Striped Pyjamas,\" \"The Theory of Everything,\" \"Black Beauty,\" and \"Wonder Woman.\"\n- David Thewlis played King Duncan in the film \"Macbeth.\"\n- King Duncan is a fictional character in William Shakespeare's \"Macbeth.\"\n- King Duncan is the father of two sons, Malcolm and Donalbain.\n- King Duncan is murdered in a regicide carried out by his trusted captain Macbeth as part of Macbeth's seizure of power.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "b9dd8216-fa4c-5766-bdb3-fd04dc8ac3db",
        "text": "Page: David Thewlis. Sentence: Other notable performances include the films \"Naked\" (for which he won the Best Actor award at Cannes Film Festival), \"Dragonheart\", \"Kingdom of Heaven\", \"The Boy in the Striped Pyjamas\", \"The Theory of Everything\", \"Black Beauty\", \"Macbeth\" (as King Duncan) and \"Wonder Woman\".\nPage: King Duncan. Sentence: King Duncan is a fictional character in Shakespeare's \"Macbeth.\"\nPage: King Duncan. Sentence: He is the father of two youthful sons (Malcolm and Donalbain), and the victim of a well-plotted regicide in a power grab by his trusted captain Macbeth.\nPage: Resurrected (film). Sentence: Resurrected is a 1989 drama film directed by Paul Greengrass in his directorial debut, written by Martin Allen, and starring David Thewlis.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
